      transcriptEN: "The excitation table is the same engine flip-flop conversion leaned on: for a chosen type it answers what input forces a specific Q(t) to Q(t+1) jump. D is trivial - D equals the next value. T asks only whether to toggle. JK and SR carry real don't-cares, the flexibility a K-map exploits. Turning an assigned table into excitation equations is bookkeeping: for every row and every flip-flop bit, look up what that bit's excitation table demands and write a new column - reusing the same ffExcite function earlier modules used, never a hand-typed answer. Under binary assignment, each product term must AND together every present-state bit plus the input, to pin down exactly which state. Under one-hot, a term needs only that one source flip-flop's output ANDed with the input - no other bit's complement required. Pick a flip-flop type and a scheme below and watch the master table and equation regenerate live, next to the flip-flop's own excitation table for reference. This is the last algebra step before silicon - AND and OR gates feeding the flip-flop pins, one shared master clock.",
